The last thing either program does is write here.

Nothing has run yet -- phase 1 has not been built -- so no program has said
goodbye. This file is the shape of the one that will.

--

What a goodbye should carry, when there is a program to write one:

  how long it ran
  where the view was left, so it can be resumed there
  which block was selected
  which filters were active, with their angles and modes
  the hour
  what was written, and to where
  what was refused, and why

The last line is the important one. A program that ends without saying what it
would not do leaves you to discover it later, in the wrong place, as a mystery.

Refusals belong here in the same voice as everything else: not an error dump, but
a sentence. "Would not save the network: two blocks name the same edge three
times." "Would not place a vertex: too far out to be precise about it."

--

The tracing tool's goodbye matters more than the game's, because the tracing tool
is where hours of irreplaceable hand-work live. Its goodbye should say plainly
how many blocks exist, how many are named, how much of the painting is fenced,
and where the network was written. Somebody should be able to read that one file
in the morning and know exactly where they left off.
